a concept is the essence of your design  the driver behind all of your design  moves  and it's your entire project distilled  into a single idea  today i'm going to show you the process  for developing the concept for  a library renovation that we're  currently working on in my office  hey guys welcome back to the channel if  you're new here my name is dammy and i  am a licensed architect in beautiful  vancouver vc  on this channel we talk about  architecture and design and  also some tools and strategies that can  help us have more meaningful and  fulfilling careers  in architecture all right so let's get  to the fun part in architecture  school there's a very big focus on just  the  development of your concept the concept  itself  and so your project might be a building  but it could also be an art piece or a  piece of furniture  as long as it helps reinforce your  concept  but when you're working on real projects  the concept itself is of course very  important but  the importance actually comes from its  use  and the fact that it's really a design  tool  for all the millions of decisions you  have to make on any single project  it's also great for communicating why  you're doing something to your clients  and to your consultants it's also great  for providing  an objective reference point you kind of  have to remember that  design is by nature subjective and so  when you're working in a team  you you might develop an idea but you  have to make sure that everyone on the  team  is on board with the direction before  you move on  sometimes when we have design reviews  we'll have instances where like every  single person in the room will have  their own opinions on the design  so in situations like this it's  really helpful to look back at the  concept and say  you know this approach makes sense  because  it reinforces the concept this way  rather than saying like  uh well i think this looks better in  this video i'm going to show you the  process for  developing the concept itself and in the  next video i'm going to show you  how to use that concept to develop the  design  so this particular project is a interior  renovation  for library so not only are you taking  aspects of the site  and the program to develop your concept  you're also taking  aspects of the existing building if the  existing building is  really interesting that can form a  really big part of your building's  concept  i personally like having more  constraints to work with  because the more constraints you have  the more  objective reasons you can give to why  you've designed something in a certain  way unfortunately i can't  show you any drawings or actual  renderings of the space  because it is still confidential at the  moment so  i'll just have to show you guys in  abstraction  through sketches but i'm i'm really  really  excited to show you guys photographs  probably in the next couple of months  so the project is the renovation of an  existing library  and libraries are going through a huge  transformation right now  with the digitization of books they're  becoming  more than just a place for books it's  actually becoming a community hub  where they have tons of events and  workshops  and lots of tools for people to come and  learn new skills  so for this project the librarians  wanted to take  a chunk of the space which is used to  store books  and they wanted to turn it into public  spaces like a digital media space  and a lounge and a multi-purpose room  the library has this grand  entrance this uh three-story atrium when  you walk in  and we will be blowing out this entire  area  and turning it into more public space  we're also renovating other areas like  this  info center and in the next phase of the  project we're also putting in a new  children's library  a new teens library and a heller upstair  that connects the two different levels  the first step to any design process is  gathering and analyzing all of the data  on your project so usually you can  divide the research into three  categories  the client or the design brief the site  and the building typology the client  usually includes  information about the client and the  building requirements  the accommodation the construction  budget and the site  will have information about uh the  neighborhood  or the climate the orientation views  access  context history use etc etc and the  building typology  is of course the type and use um and in  our case it's the library  so the client for us was the city of  who is paying for the project but the  librarians  are actually our real clients they're  the ones using the space and they're the  ones who know  what works and what doesn't work so the  very first step  of every design process is  not developing the concept and it's  not even trying to understand what the  client wants it's actually  helping the client discover and  understand  what they want you like cheesecake  i don't like cheesecake you like  cheesecake oh i guess i do like  cheesecake have you ever seen star wars  what obi-wan kenobi is talking just  imperial guards and just making that  repeating this sentence  all the time these aren't the droids  you're looking for  these aren't the droids we're looking  for he can go about his business  have you ever seen that you're seeing  star wars  usually you need to come in as the  expert  and inform them on the best practices  of that particular building typology for  us  a lot of that was collecting a bunch of  precedents  from our last project and from projects  from other firms  and also giving them a sense of where  libraries are headed in the future  and what kind of programs can kind of  future proof  the library i think libraries are one of  the most  interesting typologies i'm probably a  little bit biased because  i did do my master's thesis on libraries  and library renovations  but it's a typology that's a direct  reflection  of society's relationship with  information  and knowledge for example during the  dark ages  libraries were just repositories for  books the books  would literally be chained to the desks  it would only be accessible to a few  people who could read  and now there's less focus on  physical books and there is more focus  on the services  and the real social interactions they're  providing big  open spaces rather than small reading  nooks if you're  interested in libraries i would highly  highly recommend the book  library at night it's just a beautifully  written book on the history and the  meaning  behind libraries i'll leave the link in  the description below  and i also think librarians are some of  the most  interesting people and like the best  clients they're very organized they're  very articulated  and they would consolidate and gather  the comments  and we get proper timely feedback  all typed up in a word document  during this process we held discovery  sessions where we  try to get as much input as possible  from the librarians  on how they envisioned the space we  heard things like they want a flexible  and adaptable space accessible and  inclusive for everyone a space that  inspires  to create a space to collaborate a space  for  intergenerational connections a space  that accommodates  the different user activities flexible  spaces  and a space that celebrates the  diversity of the community the library  is located in a neighborhood where  the population is 62 immigrants  with only 45 percent of the residents  actually speaking english at home  the other thing we notice is that the  buildings  located in a very very dense area  it's an area that's being heavily  developed right now  and the library kind of sits in the  midst of all these super  tall skyscrapers the library has a  massive garden so this building  gives a bit of a breathing space to this  super dense area and it becomes a little  garden in this concrete jungle at the  scale of the building  this also reflects the nature of the  library  we have all of these super dense  programs  and we also have this tall three-story  atrium  that has this solid void relationship so  one of the strategies that i like to use  in coming up with the concept  is looking at the information and trying  to relate  aspects of it back to a  original source  it's a little tricky because there's no  hard definition  of an original source because everything  is kind of built on each other  but a good original source are icons  or visuals that are recognizable across  different cultures  when we're talking about architecture  specifically anything that's become  widespread enough to become a kind of  typology  could be considered an original source i  looked at this strong solid void  relationship  and the first thing that came to my mind  was a cave  [Music]  there's a lot of interesting material  tones that we could  pull from this but i i scrapped this  idea  pretty quickly because it didn't really  work at a programmatic or functional  level  so going back to the analysis they also  kept talking about their desire  to build a community hub and to bring  indigenous identity  into the space and so i thought of the  native longhouse  which is a traditional typology and  it's usually the heart of indigenous  communities  they would hold religious ceremonies and  have  community gatherings and this is also  where  they have the fire pit and this kind of  gives it  the opening at the roof which is  somewhat similar to our condition i  think  there's a interesting programmatic  connection  but i just felt like there weren't  enough architectural elements i could  pull from this concept to develop a  rich material palette  and so i thought about what about a  community living room  this is a key phrase they kept talking  about  is there anything i could pull from the  typology of living rooms  and so i thought about bringing in some  of these more  domestic elements like carpets and  cushions and area rugs  but in this case i felt like there  wasn't enough consistency  in the type for it to become a strong  concept  then i went for a walk and sat in this  little courtyard  and spent some time pondering my life  choices  what if we included a fountain feature  in the atrium space  it could be like an outdoor courtyard  the librarians were really adamant about  bringing  nature into the space how feasible would  that be  in this case the atrium and the site  both had the solid void relationship  both a void within a solid  a donut  the courtyard is a design trend that is  universal across different cultures and  throughout history  and it's a design element that hasn't  really gone out of style  while a lot of design trends have come  and gone based on  changes in living styles  they were originally conceived to  control the microclimatic conditions  within buildings  but their functions have evolved over  time being used for  social activities like cooking washing  sleeping gathering or socializing  this seemed to fit perfectly with the  atrium of the library  which is a space that connects a lot of  these different distinct functions  together  and serves as the main circulation  corridor  the courtyard works on a programmatic  level  it works on a physical level it works on  a contextual level  and at a material level it's it  it creates an interesting material  palette and it will  fit into all of the different areas and  elements of the project  so this is one of the strategies that i  use to develop  a concept and of course the  process looks pretty different based on  the project because each project  is unique one thing that is consistent  throughout all the design phases and  all projects is how much we look for  inspiration  from other sources you know i used to  think that good ideas come from deep  inside your head because i didn't want  to  accidentally copy something that i'd  seen on the internet  but you know what there's no original  creations  everything is basically a different  iteration of another thing  when you think of the romantic image of  an architect  you think of them drawing day and night  on their drafting board  coming up with these crazy brilliant  ideas  but in reality a huge chunk of our time  is actually spent looking at books  magazines  and the internet for inspiration the  internet if you know how to use it  properly  is really like the best place  for creating connections and  serendipitous connections  anyways i hope that was interesting for  you guys i  presented this concept to the team and  to the client and  they were on board so in the next video  i'm going to talk about how you can  translate some of these concepts  into the design and how you can use this  to drive every little decision in your  project  if you enjoyed this video please give me  a thumbs up and  let me know if you have any questions  and let me know if you have  any other strategies that you like to  use for developing concepts  bye see in the next video  you
 

